{# The toolbar: the window's title bar, on every page that has one.

   Included by base.jinja and by the painter pages, which do not extend base
   because the painter owns the rest of their document. Everything it needs
   is in ds.css, which each of them loads; it reads current_user,
   draft_post_count, unread_notification_count and ftl_lang, and nothing
   else, so a page can show it by passing those four. #}
{# Light, dark or the system's: the same switch as the painter's Dark button,
   stored under the same name, so the two never disagree. Chosen here, it
   applies at once and to every page after (theme_head.jinja).

   Under it, the language, for the signed out as much as the signed in: kept
   in a cookie, and on the account too when there is one (web/language.rs).
   A form rather than a script, since a page in a new language is a new
   document anyway; unboosted, so it is one, <html lang> and all. Each name
   is written in its own language, which is how anyone who cannot read the
   page they are on finds theirs. #}
{% macro preference_choices() %}
  <div class="toolbar-theme">
    <span class="toolbar-theme-label">{{ ftl_get_message("theme") }}</span>
    <div class="ds-segmented" role="group" aria-label="{{ ftl_get_message("theme") }}">
      <button type="button" data-theme-choice="light" aria-pressed="false">{{ ftl_get_message("theme-light") }}</button>
      <button type="button" data-theme-choice="dark" aria-pressed="false">{{ ftl_get_message("theme-dark") }}</button>
      <button type="button" data-theme-choice="system" aria-pressed="false">{{ ftl_get_message("theme-system") }}</button>
    </div>
    <form class="toolbar-language" action="/language" method="post" hx-boost="false">
      <label class="toolbar-theme-label" for="toolbar-language">{{ ftl_get_message("language") }}</label>
      {# Signed in, what the account says. Signed out the server does not
         know whether the page is in this language because it was chosen or
         because the browser asked for it, so the language in use is marked,
         and the script below moves the mark to Auto when nothing was
         chosen. #}
      {% set chosen = (current_user.preferred_language|lower if current_user.preferred_language else "auto") if current_user else ftl_lang %}
      <select class="ds-select" id="toolbar-language" name="language" onchange="this.form.submit()"{% if not current_user %} data-guest{% endif %}>
        <option value="auto"{% if chosen == "auto" %} selected{% endif %}>{{ ftl_get_message("account-language-auto") }}</option>
        {% for code, name in [("ko", "한국어"), ("ja", "日本語"), ("en", "English"), ("zh", "中文")] %}
          <option value="{{ code }}" lang="{{ code }}"{% if chosen == code %} selected{% endif %}>{{ name }}</option>
        {% endfor %}
      </select>
      {% if not current_user %}
        <script>
          (function () {
            if (!/(?:^|;\s*)lang=/.test(document.cookie)) {
              document.getElementById("toolbar-language").value = "auto";
            }
          })();
        </script>
      {% endif %}
      <noscript><button type="submit" class="ds-button ds-button-small">{{ ftl_get_message("account-language-settings-save") }}</button></noscript>
    </form>
  </div>
{% endmacro %}
{# Boost scoped to the nav, not the body.
   A boosted response has its whole <head> discarded except the title,
   and htmx re-runs swapped scripts by cloning the tag — which does not
   re-evaluate a <script type="module"> the browser has already loaded,
   because the module map is keyed on the URL and `cachebuster` holds it
   fixed for a deploy. Body-wide boost therefore leaves the painter
   unmounted the *second* time someone opens a drawing page in a session.
   Measured, not inferred.

   Scoping it here keeps every link that reaches a module bundle out of
   it: they all live in page bodies, outside this nav. The one exception
   is the draw form below, which says so itself. #}
{# The window's title bar: the sections as its tabs, Draw and the
   account at its end. The desktop app draws its window controls over
   this bar rather than above it, so the site and the window read as one
   surface; in a browser it is simply the top of the page. There is no
   separate "app mode" -- what would look out of place in the app is
   changed for everybody.

   Written for Firefox 56 like the rest of static/style.css, which no
   build step touches: flex without `gap`, and the account menu is a
   <details>, so it opens with no script at all. #}
{# data-signed-in is how the apps are told whether anyone is (app_bridge.jinja).

   data-window-drag says this bar moves the window in the desktop apps: a
   press on its empty space drags it and a double click zooms it, while its
   links, buttons and fields stay the page's. On the Mac the page reads it
   itself and asks the app to move the window (theme_head.jinja, a `window`
   message on the bridge); the Windows app's webview (Tauri) reads its own
   spelling of the same thing, data-tauri-drag-region="deep", where "deep" is what
   lets the bar's children drag too. Both are in the markup, so they arrive
   with every page and every boosted swap, and nothing in an app has to go
   looking for this bar by its class. #}
<nav class="nav-bar" hx-boost:inherited="true" data-window-drag data-tauri-drag-region="deep"{% if current_user %} data-signed-in data-user-id="{{ current_user.id }}"{% endif %}>
  <div id="menubar">
    {# The mark -- the cucumber slice the site's icon is -- and the site's
       name beside it, in the page's language: home. Its box is the buttons'
       height and it is centred on their line, which in the desktop app on
       macOS is the line the window's own controls sit on, 26px down. #}
    <a class="toolbar-name" href="/">
      <img class="toolbar-mark" src="/static/favicon.png" alt="" width="22" height="22" />
      <span class="toolbar-name-text">{{ ftl_get_message("brand") }}</span>
    </a>
    {# The sections, as a window's title bar holds its tabs. On a phone
       they take a second row of the bar, sharing its width. #}
    <div class="toolbar-links">
      <div class="toolbar-sections">
        {# Home is all of the feeds -- Recent, Following, Communities:
           which one is a switch on the page (feed_switch.jinja). #}
        <a href="/">{{ ftl_get_message("nav-home") }}</a>
        <a href="/communities">{{ ftl_get_message("community") }}</a>
        <a href="/tags">{{ ftl_get_message("tag-discovery") }}</a>
      </div>
      {# Drawing together, live: its own button beside the sections rather
         than one of them, with a pulsing light in the mark's green, since what is behind it
         is happening now. Shown signed out too: the lobby is a public
         gallery of finished collaborative drawings, and only creating a
         session needs an account. #}
      <a class="toolbar-live" href="/collaborate"><span class="toolbar-live-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>{{ ftl_get_message("collaborate") }}</a>
    </div>
    <div class="toolbar-end">
      {# Search, signed in or out, and first: the glass's own square button at rest,
         which widens leftward into a field when anything gives the field
         focus -- a press on the glass, a Tab into it, or `/` -- and closes
         again when it loses it. The glass stays at its left as it goes,
         and the corners stay the 6px every other control in the bar has:
         the width is the only thing that changes. A word here is the width that pushes a phone's first row
         into its compact steps; the pill takes none of the bar's, since it
         opens over the bar rather than in it (ds.css).

         Enter goes to /search, which is still where results are read and
         where the apps' own search fields land: this is a faster way to a
         field than going there to find one, not a second search.

         No script holds any of it up. The glass is the field's <label>, so
         pressing it focuses the field; the pill opens on :focus-within;
         and a form with a single field submits on Enter by itself. #}
      {# Unboosted, like the two forms below and for a plainer reason than
         theirs: htmx 4 takes a boosted form's submit, sends the request --
         GET /search?q=… , 200, the right page -- and swaps nothing in. The
         bar stays where it was and the search appears to do nothing. So
         this goes the way a form went before htmx existed, which is also
         the way it goes with no script at all; the skeleton is laid by
         hand below, where a boosted link would have had one for free. #}
      <form class="toolbar-search" method="get" action="/search" role="search" hx-boost="false">
        <span class="toolbar-search-pill">
          <label class="toolbar-search-glass" for="toolbar-search-field" title="{{ ftl_get_message("search") }} (/)">
            <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="10.5" cy="10.5" r="6.5"/><path d="M15.5 15.5 21 21"/></svg>
          </label>
          <input class="toolbar-search-field"
                 id="toolbar-search-field"
                 type="search"
                 name="q"
                 placeholder="{{ ftl_get_message("search-placeholder") }}"
                 aria-label="{{ ftl_get_message("search-placeholder") }}"
                 autocomplete="off"
                 autocapitalize="none"
                 spellcheck="false" />
        </span>
      </form>
      {# The Supporter Pack: a heart, after search, leading to /supporter.
         For someone signed in only: the pack is bought against an account,
         and the bar signed out is already asking for something else.
         Someone reading without one meets it after joining, on the page
         rather than in the bar. Which store can take the money is the
         page's business (style.css). Left out of the markup entirely when
         this deployment has no pack for sale.

         Drawn as its neighbours are: no fill, a 2px round stroke, 16px in
         a 30px square (ds.css, .toolbar-square). The filled weight in
         this bar is the Draw button's, and belongs to the one control that
         is filled in. #}
      {% if supporter_packs_on_sale and current_user %}
        <a class="toolbar-square toolbar-button toolbar-supporter" href="/supporter"
           aria-label="{{ ftl_get_message("supporter-pack") }}" title="{{ ftl_get_message("supporter-pack") }}">
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 20.3 5.2 13.5a4.5 4.5 0 0 1 6.8-5.9 4.5 4.5 0 0 1 6.8 5.9z"/></svg>
        </a>
      {% endif %}
      {% if current_user %}
        {% include "nav_notifications.jinja" %}
      {% else %}
        {# What the site is, first: signed in it is the last thing in the
           account menu, but signed out the only other square at the bar's
           end is the theme's, and nobody looks under a sun for what a site
           is. An info mark, square like its neighbours, with its words for
           its label and tooltip.

           Then signing in, as a mark: a door with the arrow going in.
           Draw, after it, is the filled button signed out as it is signed
           in -- a guest can draw, and keeps the drawing in this browser.
           Signing up has no button of its own: the sign in page offers it,
           so one button covers both. Both marks are Material Symbols
           (info-outline, login; Apache 2.0; THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md). #}
        <a class="toolbar-square toolbar-button toolbar-about" href="/about" aria-label="{{ ftl_get_message("nav-about-menu") }}" title="{{ ftl_get_message("nav-about-menu") }}"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M11 17h2v-6h-2zm1-8q.425 0 .713-.288T13 8t-.288-.712T12 7t-.712.288T11 8t.288.713T12 9m0 13q-2.075 0-3.9-.788t-3.175-2.137T2.788 15.9T2 12t.788-3.9t2.137-3.175T8.1 2.788T12 2t3.9.788t3.175 2.137T21.213 8.1T22 12t-.788 3.9t-2.137 3.175t-3.175 2.138T12 22m0-2q3.35 0 5.675-2.325T20 12t-2.325-5.675T12 4T6.325 6.325T4 12t2.325 5.675T12 20m0-8"/></svg></a>
        <a class="toolbar-square toolbar-button toolbar-sign-in" href="/login" aria-label="{{ ftl_get_message("sign-in") }}" title="{{ ftl_get_message("sign-in") }}"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M12 21v-2h7V5h-7V3h7q.825 0 1.413.588T21 5v14q0 .825-.587 1.413T19 21zm-2-4l-1.375-1.45l2.55-2.55H3v-2h8.175l-2.55-2.55L10 7l5 5z"/></svg></a>
        {# Drawings saved in this browser while signed out, which is the
           only place they are: a square of its own, always there so the
           bar is one width whether or not there are any. While there are,
           it lights up as 넷캔 does, with 넷캔's own pulsing light on its
           corner, and the count is in its label; the script at the foot of
           this nav does both. Beside Draw, whose work it holds. Unboosted
           for the reason the account menu's link is. A page with a folded
           corner, stroked like the bell beside it. #}
        <a class="toolbar-square toolbar-button toolbar-drafts" href="/posts/drafts" hx-boost="false" data-server-count="0" aria-label="{{ ftl_get_message("drafts") }}" title="{{ ftl_get_message("drafts") }}"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5.5 2.5h9l5 5v14h-14z"/><path d="M14.5 2.5v5h5"/></svg><span class="toolbar-live-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></a>
      {% endif %}
        {# The one control in this nav that loads a module bundle.
           Boosting it would mount the painter once per session and never
           again. Signed out too: a guest can draw, and the drawing is kept
           in this browser until they have an account to post it from.

           Only its brush, square like the icons beside it: its words are
           its label and tooltip. It keeps the lime fill, the one filled
           control in the bar. #}
        <form action="/draw" method="post" hx-boost="false">
          <input type="hidden" name="tool" value="neo" />
          <input type="hidden" name="width" value="300" />
          <input type="hidden" name="height" value="300" />
          <button type="submit" id="nav-draw-button" class="toolbar-square toolbar-draw" title="{{ ftl_get_message('draw') }}" aria-label="{{ ftl_get_message('draw') }}">
            {# material-symbols:brush (Apache 2.0; THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md). #}<svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="currentColor" d="M6 21q-1.125 0-2.225-.55T2 19q.65 0 1.325-.513T4 17q0-1.25.875-2.125T7 14t2.125.875T10 17q0 1.65-1.175 2.825T6 21m5.75-6L9 12.25l8.95-8.95q.275-.275.688-.288t.712.288l1.35 1.35q.3.3.3.7t-.3.7z"/></svg>
          </button>
        </form>
      {% if current_user %}
        <details class="toolbar-menu">
          <summary aria-label="{{ current_user.display_name or current_user.login_name }}">
            {# The initial pulses green while there are drafts left to
               finish, and an unverified email puts a red dot on it. Drafts
               used to be a button in the bar while there were any, which
               changed the bar's width -- and so whether it fit one row on a
               phone -- from one visit to the next. #}
            <span class="toolbar-square toolbar-avatar{% if draft_post_count > 0 %} toolbar-avatar-pulse{% endif %}" aria-hidden="true">{{ (current_user.display_name or current_user.login_name or "?")[:1]|upper }}</span>
            {% if not current_user.email_verified_at %}<span class="toolbar-dot" title="{{ ftl_get_message('email-not-verified') }}"></span>{% endif %}
          </summary>
          <div class="toolbar-menu-items">
            <div class="toolbar-menu-who">
              <strong>{{ current_user.display_name }}</strong>
              <span>@{{ current_user.login_name }}</span>
            </div>
            <a href="/@{{ current_user.login_name }}">{{ ftl_get_message("profile") }}</a>
            {# Unboosted: the drafts page lists the drawings kept in this
               browser with a module bundle, which a boosted visit would not
               run a second time. The script below adds those to the count. #}
            <a href="/posts/drafts" hx-boost="false" class="toolbar-menu-drafts" data-server-count="{{ draft_post_count }}">{{ ftl_get_message("drafts") }}
              {%- if draft_post_count > 0 %} <span class="toolbar-badge">{{ draft_post_count }}</span>{% endif -%}
            </a>
            <a href="/account">{{ ftl_get_message("account") }}
              {% if not current_user.email_verified_at %}
                <span class="dangerous">({{ ftl_get_message("email-not-verified") }})</span>
              {% endif %}
            </a>
            {# Signing out: here rather than at the foot of /account, where
               it was, because it belongs beside the name it ends. A POST,
               so it is a form and not a link; unboosted, so the signed out
               document is a fresh one rather than a body swapped into the
               signed in page's head. #}
            <form action="/logout" method="post" hx-boost="false">
              <button type="submit" class="toolbar-menu-signout">{{ ftl_get_message("sign-out") }}</button>
            </form>
            {# The menu's foot, the same signed in or out: the theme, then
               a rule, then what the site is. #}
            {{ preference_choices() }}
            <a class="toolbar-menu-about" href="/about">{{ ftl_get_message("nav-about-menu") }}</a>
          </div>
        </details>
      {% else %}
        {# Where your initial would be, the theme in use, opening onto the
           three-way switch and the language and nothing else: what the
           site is and this browser's drafts have their own buttons in the
           bar above, since a theme square is not where anyone would look
           for either. #}
        <details class="toolbar-menu toolbar-theme-menu">
          <summary aria-label="{{ ftl_get_message("theme-and-language") }}" title="{{ ftl_get_message("theme-and-language") }}">
            <span class="toolbar-square toolbar-avatar toolbar-avatar-guest" aria-hidden="true">
              <svg class="toolbar-theme-light" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="4"/><path d="M12 2.5v2M12 19.5v2M2.5 12h2M19.5 12h2M5.3 5.3l1.4 1.4M17.3 17.3l1.4 1.4M5.3 18.7l1.4-1.4M17.3 6.7l1.4-1.4"/></svg>
              <svg class="toolbar-theme-dark" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M20 14.5A8 8 0 0 1 9.5 4 8 8 0 1 0 20 14.5Z"/></svg>
              <svg class="toolbar-theme-system" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="8.5"/><path d="M12 3.5a8.5 8.5 0 0 1 0 17Z" fill="currentColor"/></svg>
            </span>
          </summary>
          <div class="toolbar-menu-items">
            {{ preference_choices() }}
          </div>
        </details>
      {% endif %}
    </div>
  </div>
  {# The keyboard shortcuts, listed. Hidden until `?` or the desktop app's
     Help menu asks for it; see oeeeApp.command below. #}
  <div class="ds-shortcuts" id="ds-shortcuts" hidden>
    <div class="ds-shortcuts-panel" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="ds-shortcuts-title">
      <div class="ds-shortcuts-head">
        <h2 id="ds-shortcuts-title">{{ ftl_get_message("keyboard-shortcuts") }}</h2>
        <button type="button" class="ds-button ds-button-quiet ds-button-small" data-shortcuts-close>{{ ftl_get_message("close") }}</button>
      </div>
      <dl>
        {% if current_user %}
          <dt><kbd>N</kbd></dt><dd>{{ ftl_get_message("draw") }}</dd>
        {% endif %}
        <dt><kbd>/</kbd></dt><dd>{{ ftl_get_message("search") }}</dd>
        <dt><kbd>G</kbd> {{ ftl_get_message("keyboard-shortcuts-then") }} <kbd>R</kbd></dt><dd>{{ ftl_get_message("nav-home") }}</dd>
        {% if current_user %}
          <dt><kbd>G</kbd> {{ ftl_get_message("keyboard-shortcuts-then") }} <kbd>F</kbd></dt><dd>{{ ftl_get_message("feed-following") }}</dd>
        {% endif %}
        <dt><kbd>G</kbd> {{ ftl_get_message("keyboard-shortcuts-then") }} <kbd>C</kbd></dt><dd>{{ ftl_get_message("community") }}</dd>
        <dt><kbd>G</kbd> {{ ftl_get_message("keyboard-shortcuts-then") }} <kbd>T</kbd></dt><dd>{{ ftl_get_message("collaborate") }}</dd>
        <dt><kbd>G</kbd> {{ ftl_get_message("keyboard-shortcuts-then") }} <kbd>H</kbd></dt><dd>{{ ftl_get_message("tag-discovery") }}</dd>
        {% if current_user %}
          <dt><kbd>G</kbd> {{ ftl_get_message("keyboard-shortcuts-then") }} <kbd>N</kbd></dt><dd>{{ ftl_get_message("notifications") }}</dd>
          <dt><kbd>G</kbd> {{ ftl_get_message("keyboard-shortcuts-then") }} <kbd>D</kbd></dt><dd>{{ ftl_get_message("drafts") }}</dd>
          <dt><kbd>G</kbd> {{ ftl_get_message("keyboard-shortcuts-then") }} <kbd>P</kbd></dt><dd>{{ ftl_get_message("profile") }}</dd>
        {% endif %}
        <dt><kbd>?</kbd></dt><dd>{{ ftl_get_message("keyboard-shortcuts-show") }}</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>
  {# Which item is the page you are on: the server does not pass the
     request path to templates, so this reads it here. The boost swaps
     the whole body, this included, so it runs again on every visit;
     the document listeners, which outlive a swap, are added once.

     And the account menu closes the way a menu does, on a click
     anywhere else and on Escape, which <details> alone does not. #}
  <script>
    (function () {
      // Also called with the path being gone to, the moment it is asked for,
      // so the tab moves on the click rather than when the page arrives --
      // and with the page's own path again if it never does.
      var HOME = /^\/(following|joined)?$/;
      window.oeeeMarkCurrent = function (here) {
        var links = document.querySelectorAll("#menubar a[href]");
        for (var i = 0; i < links.length; i++) {
          var path = links[i].getAttribute("href");
          // Home is every one of its feeds (feed_switch.jinja).
          if (path === "/" ? HOME.test(here) : here === path || here.indexOf(path + "/") === 0) {
            links[i].setAttribute("aria-current", "page");
          } else {
            links[i].removeAttribute("aria-current");
          }
        }
      };
      window.oeeeMarkCurrent(location.pathname);

      // One row, or two: try the name, the sections and the account on one
      // line, and if the account or the sections wrapped below the name --
      // or would sit closer than 16px to the sections -- give the sections a
      // row of their own (ds.css, .is-stacked). Measured rather than set at
      // a width, because the width that fits depends on the language and,
      // in the desktop app, on the window's own controls.
      window.oeeeFitToolbar = function () {
        var nav = document.querySelector(".nav-bar");
        var bar = document.getElementById("menubar");
        if (!nav || !bar) return;
        var name = bar.querySelector(".toolbar-name");
        var links = bar.querySelector(".toolbar-links");
        var end = bar.querySelector(".toolbar-end");
        if (!name || !links || !end) return;
        nav.classList.remove("is-stacked", "is-compact", "is-bare");
        var line = name.getBoundingClientRect();
        var sections = links.getBoundingClientRect();
        var account = end.getBoundingClientRect();
        var oneRow = sections.top < line.bottom && account.top < line.bottom &&
          account.left - sections.right >= 16;
        if (oneRow) return;
        nav.classList.add("is-stacked");
        // Stacked, the first line still has to hold the name and the
        // account. If it does not, first Draw keeps only its pencil and the
        // buttons close up; then, if even that wraps, the name steps aside
        // and the mark stays, still the way home. Each step only where it is
        // needed.
        function wrapped() {
          return end.getBoundingClientRect().top >= name.getBoundingClientRect().bottom;
        }
        if (!wrapped()) return;
        nav.classList.add("is-compact");
        if (!wrapped()) return;
        nav.classList.add("is-bare");
      };
      window.oeeeFitToolbar();
      // The content area scrolls, not the window, and browsers only restore
      // the window's own scroll when you go back -- so where you were is
      // kept here, per page, and put back on a full load that came from the
      // history. Going back within the site is htmx's history cache
      // (base.jinja), which keeps every scrolled element's place itself.
      // And the content area takes the keyboard's focus when
      // nothing else has it, so Page Down scrolls it without a click first.
      // The toolbar comes before <main> in the page, so on a full load this
      // waits for it; after a boosted swap it is already there.
      function settleContent() {
        var content = document.querySelector("main.ds-content");
        if (!content) return;
        var key = "oeee-scroll:" + location.pathname + location.search;
        var entry = window.performance && performance.getEntriesByType
          ? performance.getEntriesByType("navigation")[0] : null;
        var returning = !window.oeeeSettledOnce && entry && entry.type === "back_forward";
        window.oeeeSettledOnce = true;
        if (returning) {
          try {
